from the very beginning of the program  we've we've been faced with the question  of how to define a successful society  but we also quickly agreed that we were  not going to try to come up with some  magic formula the argument we've  developed in our work is really a  complex argument in the sense that we  each look at different parts of the  animal so our response to us a  successful Society has both an  institutional part which you know what  does the state does redistribute  resources etc but it also has a cultural  part which is about recognition how do  the societies we live in give us tools  to build identities that are strong and  positive I would look at that in terms  of what type of society is going to help  people meet their needs and so what kind  of needs to people have people have a  need for safety and security we need  water we need food we need shelter we  need clothing social needs  right so relatedness and belonging a  sense of inclusion from my point of view  a successful society is one that  provides people with us certain level of  prosperity decent jobs at a living wage  for everyone society that distributes  the fruits of that growth relatively  equitably but also a society that is  tolerant where people are tolerant of  are those in other religious racial or  ethnic groups strong institutions are  primary social networks and the trust in  those institutions at the individual  level autonomy being able to learn and  to think critically being able to make  responsible decisions and  to enter live by them and to behave  accordingly I mean there are many  features of a successful society and I  think the important thing to realize is  that there are often trade-offs there  you can't always have all good things  all the time sometimes to have more of  one good thing you have to have a little  bit less of something else and so there  are trade-offs that's one of the great  things about human beings is we're  endlessly active and creative and and  we're never we're never satisfied with  we live in and and there will there will  always be people mobilizing to in in to  pursue a better vision of what what the  world looks like
 

